// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// nova-http: middleware.nv — `polaris.middleware` composition core (Plan
// 222.4). Basis: Go/`chi` — their `func(http.Handler) http.Handler` is our
// `fn(next Handler) -> Handler`, ONE-TO-ONE (plan banner). Tower's generic
// `Service` (poll_ready/backpressure) is explicitly NOT ported (plan §4):
// our sync fiber model has no async readiness signal to speak of, and
// backpressure already lives at the accept-loop (222.7 admission-bounded).
//
// `Middleware` is a BARE newtype over the higher-order fn type (`type
// Middleware fn(Handler) -> Handler`), uniform with `Handler` (server.nv:507)
// — migrated off the record-wrapper shape 2026-07-24 (p-mw-bare2 worktree)
// once `[M-nested-fn-newtype-bind-then-call-broken]` (окно №78) closed in
// nova main: that defect (nested newtype-over-fn bound to a `ro` local via a
// call/method result or closure-capture then called/composed further) was
// the actual blocker for the bare shape, NOT №47/[M-vec-of-fn-newtype-codegen]
// (closed earlier, insufficient alone — see the prior audit commit on this
// branch). Composition methods (`@apply`/`@then`) hang off the newtype same
// as before (D84 property style throughout); `@apply` is now a direct
// call-through of the newtype VALUE (`@(next)`), not a field-deref.
//
// File placement: peer of `server.nv`/`server_router.nv` in the SAME module
// (`http.server`) — folder-module peers compile in sorted order (D78).
// `middleware.nv` sorts BEFORE both (`m` < `s`) — this is the CORRECT
// direction for the one real embed-by-value this file introduces: `Router`
// (server_router.nv) gets a NEW `mut layers []Middleware` field below, so
// `Middleware` must already be complete by the time `server_router.nv`'s
// `Router` struct is declared — which this ordering guarantees. The
// reverse concern (`Middleware`'s OWN field, `fn(Handler) -> Handler`, needs
// `Handler` from `server.nv`, which sorts AFTER this file) is the same
// "precautionary, not a hard requirement" situation `extract.nv`'s banner
// documents for its own use of `Router`/`ServerRequest` — a closure/fn-type
// FIELD is not the same hazard as embedding another module's STRUCT by
// value as a new member (`MethodEntry.handler`/`RouteNode.methods` do that,
// which is why `server_router.nv` itself must sort after `server.nv`); this
// compiled clean, so the concern doesn't materialize here either.
//
// ── Router integration (edits in `server_router.nv`: `Router.layers` field +
// `Router.@layer`) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layers accumulate on `Router.@layers` and are baked into each
// `MethodRouter`'s handlers INSIDE `@insert_segs` — i.e. at REGISTRATION
// time (`@route`/`@get`/…/`@nest` all funnel through `@insert_segs`), NOT at
// `@dispatch` (cheaper: one closure-wrap per route at setup, not one per
// request — plan §2).
//
// Order — REAL-chi semantics (this plan-run's explicit resolution of the
// plan draft's own admitted confusion, plan §2: "частый источник путаницы").
// The plan's illustrative text there ("r.layer(a); r.layer(b) → запрос идёт
// b → a → handler", i.e. LATER-added = outer) does NOT match actual `chi`
// behavior: `chi`'s `chain()` (middleware.go) builds
// `h := mws[last](endpoint)` then wraps OUTWARD with `mws[i]` for
// `i = len-2 downTo 0` — `mws[0]` (FIRST-registered) ends up OUTERMOST and
// runs FIRST at request time (same for Express `app.use(a); app.use(b)` —
// `a` runs first). This file implements the REAL rule: **first `.layer()`
// call = outermost, runs first**; `r.layer(a); r.layer(b)` → request flow
// is `a → b → handler`. Pin-tested in `middleware_test.nv`.
//
// `Router.@fallback` (the GLOBAL 404, set independently of `@route`/
// `@insert_segs`) is NOT wrapped by layers — it isn't a registered "route"
// in the trie at all, so per-Handler wrap-at-registration has no hook for
// it. This makes our `.layer()` behave like Axum's `route_layer` (applies
// only to routes actually registered on THIS router) rather than Axum's
// `.layer()` (which wraps the whole tower `Service`, fallback included,
// because it wraps DISPATCH as a whole — the "expensive" per-request shape
// this plan deliberately avoids, §4). `MethodRouter.@fallback` (the
// PER-ROUTE 405 override) DOES get wrapped — it is set as part of building
// the very `MethodRouter` that `@route`/`@insert_segs` registers, same as
// any GET/POST handler on that route. Both choices are pin-tested in
// `middleware_test.nv`.
//
// `@nest`'s "parent layers on top of sub's own" (plan §3) falls out of this
// design for free: `@nest` re-inserts `sub`'s already-collected routes via
// the SAME `self.@insert_segs` (server_router.nv's `@nest` body), which
// wraps them (again) with the PARENT's current `@layers` — `sub`'s routes
// were already wrapped with SUB's own layers back when they were registered
// on `sub` (value semantics: `sub` itself is never mutated by `@nest`, see
// that file's banner), so the parent's layers land OUTSIDE. The plan's
// flagged "pitfall" (double-application if `sub` was nested elsewhere too)
// does not actually trigger under value semantics — `wrap_method_router`
// below returns a NEW `MethodRouter`, `sub`'s own tree is read-only to
// `@nest` — but a pin-test proves it (nesting the SAME sub into two parents
// with different layer sets, no cross-contamination).

module http.server

/// A middleware: wraps a `Handler` into a new `Handler` (Go/`chi`
/// `func(http.Handler) http.Handler`; Express `use`-spirit). Bare newtype
/// over the fn type (D52/D55), uniform with `Handler` (server.nv:507) —
/// migrated off the record-wrapper shape 2026-07-24 after
/// `[M-nested-fn-newtype-bind-then-call-broken]` (окно №78) closed in nova
/// main; see this file's own history for the record-wrapper rationale this
/// superseded.
#stable(since = "0.1")
export type Middleware fn(Handler) -> Handler

/// Wrap a closure as a `Middleware` (POWER form: the outer closure runs once
/// per composition — use it for middleware with one-time setup work; for the
/// common "wrap every request" case prefer the flat `middleware(...)` below,
/// 222.4 §4а canon). `-> ro Middleware`: a bare `=> f` identity-return of the
/// `ro`-bound param `f` under the (mut-default) return type hits
/// `[E_READONLY_COERCE]` (D246 Ф.1б) — the source value itself is never
/// mutated by any caller, so declaring the return `ro` here is legal and
/// avoids the coercion without a superfluous `.clone()`.
#stable(since = "0.1")
export fn Middleware.new(f fn(Handler) -> Handler) -> ro Middleware => f

/// CANON form (222.4 §4а, owner decision 2026-07-23): build a middleware from
/// ONE flat closure `fn(req, next)` — no nested `fn(next) -> Handler`
/// ceremony. `next` is a plain `Handler` value: call it directly
/// (`next(req)`), run code after it, or short-circuit by returning your own
/// response without calling it.
///
/// ```nova
/// ro logging = middleware(fn(req ServerRequest, next Handler) -> ServerResponse {
///     ro resp = next(req)
///     resp
/// })
/// ```
///
/// `Middleware` itself is now the BARE newtype over `fn(Handler) -> Handler`
/// (file banner) — this constructor stays a plain function (not a
/// `Middleware` method) purely for the flat-closure ergonomics described
/// above, unrelated to the newtype's own record-vs-bare shape.
///
/// MIGRATED 2026-07-24 (p-mw-bare2 worktree, after
/// `[M-nested-fn-newtype-bind-then-call-broken]` (окно №78) closed in nova
/// main): a PRIOR attempt (p-mw-bare-newtype worktree, after only №47
/// [M-vec-of-fn-newtype-codegen] had closed) tried this same bare-newtype
/// shape and reverted — that attempt found the real, deeper blocker
/// (`[M-nested-fn-newtype-bind-then-call-broken]`, claimed as a marker
/// there): a NESTED newtype-over-fn (`Middleware` wraps `fn(Handler) ->
/// Handler`, and `Handler` is ITSELF a newtype-over-fn) bound to a `ro`
/// local via a call/method result or closure-capture, then
/// called/composed further, broke codegen (dropped initializer, bogus
/// direct call to a nonexistent `nova_fn_<localname>`, or a linker
/// failure) — hitting exactly the shapes `@then`'s closure-capture and
/// `wrap_handler`'s per-layer bind-then-call need. With that defect now
/// fixed at the root in nova main, the bare shape is confirmed usable end-
/// to-end by this worktree's gate (see README/CHANGELOG for the gate
/// verdict) — no in-language workaround remains needed.
#stable(since = "0.1")
export fn middleware(f fn(ServerRequest, Handler) -> ServerResponse) -> Middleware {
    ro g = f
    Middleware.new(fn(next Handler) -> Handler => flat_handler(g, next))
}

// Hoisted one level per closure ([M-16] discipline, see `mark`'s old shape in
// middleware_test.nv): the returned Handler-closure captures `g`/`n` from its
// DIRECT enclosing scope and hands them to a top-level fn as PARAMS — calling
// a CAPTURED fn value by identifier is the live [M-16] mis-codegen
// (`nova_fn_g` bogus direct call), param call-through is fine (D52 кусок 3).
fn flat_handler(f fn(ServerRequest, Handler) -> ServerResponse, next Handler) -> Handler {
    ro g = f
    ro n = next
    fn(req ServerRequest) -> ServerResponse => flat_invoke(g, n, req)
}

fn flat_invoke(f fn(ServerRequest, Handler) -> ServerResponse, next Handler, req ServerRequest) -> ServerResponse =>
    f(req, next)

/// Apply this middleware to a concrete handler, producing the wrapped `Handler`.
#stable(since = "0.1")
export fn Middleware @apply(next Handler) -> Handler => @(next)

/// Compose two middlewares: `a.then(b)` — `a` wraps OUTSIDE, `b` INSIDE (`a`
/// runs first at request time, then `b`, then the eventual handler).
/// `a.then(b).apply(h) == a.apply(b.apply(h))`.
// [M-16] (detach-capture, known live compiler bug — cited per task
// instruction, not re-diagnosed): capturing a RAW `fn(Handler) -> Handler`
// local (e.g. `ro outer_f = @f`, then calling it as `outer_f(x)` inside a
// nested closure) codegens a bogus direct call to a top-level function
// literally named `nova_fn_outer_f` instead of invoking the captured
// closure value — repro was a one-line change, reverted here. Workaround:
// capture the whole `Middleware` VALUE (bare `@`, precedent:
// `std/collections/linkedlist.nv`'s `match @ { ... }`) and invoke it via
// `.apply(...)` method-call syntax (same shape as `inner.apply(next)` below,
// which codegens correctly) instead of calling a captured raw fn-value by
// identifier.
#stable(since = "0.1")
export fn Middleware @then(inner Middleware) -> Middleware {
    ro outer = @
    Middleware.new(fn(next Handler) -> Handler => outer.apply(inner.apply(next)))
}

// ── Router integration: wrap-at-registration ───────────────────────────────

// Fold `layers` around `h`. Walking the list back-to-front means the LAST
// loop iteration applies `layers[0]` around everything already built —
// `layers[0]` (first `.layer()` call) ends up as the outermost wrap, i.e.
// the one that runs first (real-chi order, see file banner).
// `h` is `mut` (D246 canon (a), in-out): every call site hands its own
// binding through exactly once and never rereads it afterward, so there is
// no need for the separate `wrapped` local this used to launder into.
fn wrap_handler(layers []Middleware, mut h Handler) -> Handler {
    mut i = layers.len() - 1
    while i >= 0 {
        ro mw = layers[i]
        h = mw.apply(h)
        i -= 1
    }
    h
}

// Wrap every method-handler AND the per-route 405 `@fallback` held by `mr`
// (both are part of "registering this route" — see file banner) with
// `layers`. A no-op (returns `mr` unchanged) when there are no layers yet —
// avoids an allocation/rebuild on the hot path for routers that never call
// `.layer()`.
// `mr` stays a bare (`ro`-bound) param here — canon (a) (`mut mr`, in-out)
// was tried and REJECTED: it cascades into `@insert_segs`/`@route`
// (server_router.nv), which is public API called with fluent RVALUE chains
// (`r.route("/x", get(h).post(h2))`, `router_test.nv`) that have no
// addressable storage to bind a `mut` param to (`E_MUT_ARG_NOT_MUTABLE`).
// (b) instead: `.clone()` (D230) the ONE early-return path that would
// otherwise hand the bare ro-bound `mr` back out to a `mut`-defaulted
// caller binding (D184) — canon (c) doesn't fit either, the return type here
// is genuinely `-> MethodRouter` (mut-default), not `-> ro MethodRouter`.
fn wrap_method_router(layers []Middleware, mr MethodRouter) -> MethodRouter {
    if layers.len() == 0 { return mr.clone() }
    mut wrapped_routes = []MethodEntry.new()
    for e in mr.routes {
        wrapped_routes.push(MethodEntry{method: e.method, handler: wrap_handler(layers, e.handler)})
    }
    // No custom 405 override? When this route is covered by layers, MATERIALIZE
    // the default `405 + Allow` as a fallback Handler and wrap it too (Plan
    // 222.12 Ф.1 amendment to the 222.4 shape). Rationale: middleware that
    // must see EVERY method hitting a registered route — CORS preflight
    // (`OPTIONS` on a GET-only route) is the canonical case — otherwise dies
    // on the dispatch-time generic 405, which no layer ever wraps. The wire
    // bytes are IDENTICAL to dispatch's own `method_not_allowed` (same fn,
    // `Allow` computed from the same method set — fixed at registration,
    // exactly when this wrap runs); routes with no layers keep `None` and the
    // dispatch-time path, byte-for-byte as before. Pin-tested in
    // middleware_test.nv ("default 405 ... IS wrapped").
    ro wrapped_fallback = match mr.fallback {
        Some(mut h) => Some(wrap_handler(layers, h))
        None    => Some(wrap_handler(layers, default_405_handler(mr.allowed_methods())))
    }
    { routes: wrapped_routes, fallback: wrapped_fallback }
}

// The registration-time twin of dispatch's `method_not_allowed` path (same
// response builder). `allow` is copied to a local before capture ([M-16]-family
// param-capture discipline, see middleware/cors.nv). The closure auto-lifts
// into `Handler` in return position (D55 fn-genus coercion) — no wrapper call.
fn default_405_handler(allow []str) -> Handler {
    ro a = allow
    fn(_req ServerRequest) -> ServerResponse => method_not_allowed(a)
}

/// Accumulate a middleware onto this router's layer chain. Baked into every
/// handler registered AFTER this call (`@route`/`@get`/…/`@nest` — all
/// funnel through `@insert_segs`, server_router.nv) — routes registered
/// BEFORE this call do NOT get it (chi's own documented rule: middlewares
/// must be added before the routes they should cover). Chainable (D409).
#stable(since = "0.1")
export fn Router mut @layer(mw Middleware) -> @ { @layers.push(mw) }
